# Reasons for volumes:
- performance -- Images use copy on write file system -- we want to reduce layers, better to use external storage for write-intensive apps
- persisting data -- containers are short lived
- data reuse betw containers, (NFS also possible but discouraged)

# volume vs bind-mount
mapping files from containers to the host machine, use with `podman run` 
- volume is managed by podman, easier 
- bind mount is a location on host or NFS

2 type syntax:
`--volume <path on host/volume name>:<path_in_container>:options` (options rw, ro, Z etc)
`--mount type=TYPE,source=<path_on_host>,destination=<p_in_cont>`
TYPE is one of bind, volume, tmpfs (temporary file system, just for perf optimization, configurable only in mount syntax)

# troubleshooting bind mounts
-- possible permission issues, check with:

`podman unshare ls -l` user namespace share (use podman's namespace that mapps external to container users)

-- possible SELinux issues, label `container_file_t` has to be on the bind mount, podman can fix it with z (accessible to all containers) or Z (exclusive to a given container) option that changes the context on the file (can have bad consequences)

--volume <src>:<dst>:z

# volumes

podman volume create <name>
podman volume import <vol_name> data.tar.gz (also export)

# providing data to database containers
podman cp file container:path
podman exec cotainer psql...
-- or use volumes and bind mounts, e.g. -v <volume>:/var/lib/pgsql/data

